oach the UI should take if benchmark counts are unavailable mid-run (elapsed time as primary indicator, per the strategy's risk mitigation).\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written determination states whether status.benchmarks[] is populated while a job is running or only upon completion, citing EvalHub Server source locations and/or observed API responses as evidence.\n- If populated mid-run, the fields available mid-run (per-benchmark status, started_at, completed_at) are documented.\n- A recommendation for the progress-display approach is produced, sufficient to finalize the scope of the benchmark progress and elapsed time display work.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Benchmark-level progress display for running jobs (supports; determines feasibility and shape)\n\n---\n\n**E002** (Investigation):\n\n# Investigate message_code values distinguishing pre-start from runtime evaluation failures\n\n## Description\nResolves what values the EvalHub Server's status.message.message_code field takes for pre-start failures (admission errors, scheduling failures, resource quota/constraints) versus runtime failures. The answer determines whether the Evaluations job list can reliably render \"Failed (not started)\" as a distinct visual treatment from runtime \"Failed\".\n\n## Scope\n- Inspect the EvalHub Server source code to enumerate the message_code values emitted for admission errors, scheduling failures, and resource constraints, and those emitted for runtime errors during benchmark execution.\n- Where feasible, corroborate with observed failure responses.\n- Document a mapping of message_code values (or ranges/prefixes) to pre-start vs runtime failure classes, or conclude that no reliable distinction exists.\n- Recommend whether the pre-start/runtime failure distinction is implementable, and the fallback (single \"Failed\" state with status.message text as supplementary detail) if not.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- A written enumeration of message_code values per failure class exists, with source citations.\n- A determination states whether the values reliably distinguish pre-start from runtime failures across error scenarios.\n- If the distinction is not reliable, the recommended fallback treatment is documented.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P2] Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction using status.message.message_code (determines feasibility and existence)\n\n---\n\n**E003** (Implementation, P0):\n\n# EvalHub Evaluations page auto-refresh polling layer and state differentiation\n\n## Description\nDelivers the real-time state visibility foundation for the EvalHub Evaluations landing page in the RHOAI dashboard: a two-layer polling architecture (existing 30-second list-endpoint polling plus a new 10-second detail-polling layer for in-progress jobs), round-robin concurrency control capped at 5 concurrent requests, Page Visibility API pause/resume, non-blocking error handling with retry and exponential backoff, and visual differentiation of queued/pending, running, failed, and completed states.\n\n## Scope\n- Detail-polling layer in the eval-hub-ui plugin that identifies jobs on the currently displayed table page in a non-terminal state and fetches GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id} on a 10-second cycle, following the React Query retry-with-backoff pattern established by the autorag package (placeholderData preserving last successful response, exponential backoff with jitter, 4xx non-retryable, 5xx/network retried).\n- Concurrency control: maximum 5 concurrent detail requests, dispatched round-robin so the next pending job is fetched as one completes; a 10-second timer starts when all detail fetches for the current page complete; only jobs visible on the current table page (client-side pagination, 5/10/20 rows) are polled, with targets updating immediately when the page changes.\n- Page Visibility API integration: polling pauses when the browser tab is backgrounded and resumes with an immediate single cycle on focus.\n- Error handling: non-blocking inline warning (PatternFly Alert variant \"warning\") indicating status updates are temporarily unavailable while continuing to display the last successfully fetched data; a failed detail request does not stop the round-robin.\n- State visual differentiation driven by the list endpoint's status.state field: distinct PatternFly label, icon, and color for queued/pending, running, failed (with status.message as error context), and completed; state badge transitions appear without manual refresh within the list polling interval.\n- Graceful degradation: if the detail endpoint is unavailable, rows show only top-level state from the list endpoint; existing list polling and table rendering continue to function unchanged (additive change).\n- Validation of the retry pattern in the eval-hub-ui Module Federation context by simulating 5xx errors from the BFF proxy.\n- Verification that the odh-dashboard BFF proxy passes through all response fields from the eval-hub-ui plugin backend without filtering (existing behavior; no BFF code change expected).\n- State icon/color specifications reviewed by UX/Design.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an evaluation job transitions from running to complete, when the user is on the Evaluations landing page without manually refreshing the browser, then the job row's state badge updates automatically within the list endpoint's 30-second polling interval.\n- Given an evaluation job is queued/pending, when a user views the Evaluations landing page, then the row displays a distinct \"Queued\" label, icon, and color treatment visually distinguishable from Running, Failed, and Completed, per the PatternFly status pattern.\n- Given the EvalHub Server is temporarily unreachable during detail polling (5xx or network error), then the last successfully fetched data remains displayed, a non-blocking inline warning is visible, the failed request retries with exponential backoff (4xx errors not retried), and the round-robin continues with the next in-progress job.\n- Given the user navigates away from the browser tab, then no requests are made to the detail endpoint while the tab is backgrounded, and a single polling cycle triggers immediately when the tab regains focus (verified via browser dev tools Network tab).\n- At most 5 detail requests are in flight concurrently per user session regardless of the number of in-progress jobs on the page, and only jobs on the currently displayed table page are polled.\n- When all detail fetches for the current page complete, the next polling cycle starts after a 10-second timer.\n- The retry-with-backoff pattern is validated in the eval-hub-ui Module Federation context against simulated 5xx responses from the BFF proxy.\n- The BFF proxy is verified to pass through all response fields from the eval-hub-ui plugin backend without filtering.\n- State icon, color, and label specifications are reviewed and approved by UX/Design.\n- The existing list-endpoint polling and table rendering continue to function unchanged with the detail-polling layer active.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P0] Auto-refresh mechanism for the Evaluations landing page job list showing state transitions without manual refresh, using existing list polling plus new detail polling\n- [P0] Visual distinction between queued/pending, running, failed, and completed states with distinct labels, icons, and colors\n- [P0] Non-blocking error handling during detail polling with last successful state preserved, inline warning, and retry with exponential backoff\n- [P1] Polling pauses when the browser tab is backgrounded (Page Visibility API) and resumes immediately on focus\n- [P1] Max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch for in-progress jobs on the current table page (polling-layer portion)\n\n---\n\n**E004** (Implementation, P1, gated):\n\n# Benchmark progress and elapsed time display for running evaluation jobs\n\n## Description\nDelivers the progress display for running evaluation jobs on the EvalHub Evaluations landing page: benchmark-level progress (\"Benchmarks: X/N complete\") derived from the detail endpoint's status.benchmarks[] array, and elapsed time computed client-side from per-benchmark started_at timestamps (falling back to resource.created_at before any benchmark has started). Consumes the detail-polling data layer introduced for the Evaluations landing page.\n\n## Scope\n- Benchmark-level progress display for running jobs: count of status.benchmarks[] entries with status \"completed\" over total benchmarks (e.g., \"Benchmarks: 2/5 complete\").\n- Elapsed time display computed client-side as the difference between current time and the earliest per-benchmark started_at across benchmarks (or resource.created_at if no benchmark has started); per-benchmark duration available where both started_at and completed_at are present.\n- Until the first detail fetch returns for a given job, only top-level state from the list endpoint is shown (no benchmark progress or elapsed time).\n- Elapsed time serves as the primary progress indicator for all jobs, with benchmark count supplementary — including single-benchmark jobs where the count alone shows only 0 or 1 complete (risk mitigation).\n- Progress indicator pattern reviewed by UX/Design; the strategy notes a progress indicator UX specification is to be created.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an evaluation job is running with 3 of 5 benchmarks completed, when the detail-polling cycle has completed for that job, then benchmark-level progress is displayed (e.g., \"Benchmarks: 3/5 complete\"), measured by the count matching the entries in status.benchmarks[] with status \"completed\" in the GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id} response.\n- Given an evaluation job has been running for 4 minutes, when the detail-polling cycle has fetched the job's detail, then elapsed time is displayed (e.g., \"Elapsed: 4m 12s\"), measured by the difference between current time and the earliest per-benchmark started_at timestamp (resource.created_at before any benchmark starts).\n- Given no detail fetch has yet returned for a job, then only top-level state from the list endpoint is displayed for that job.\n- For single-benchmark jobs, elapsed time is displayed as the primary progress indicator.\n- The progress indicator pattern is reviewed by UX/Design.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P1] Benchmark-level progress display for running jobs derived from status.benchmarks[], with max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch (display portion)\n- [P1] Elapsed time display for running jobs, computed client-side from per-benchmark started_at timestamps\n\n---\n\nWait — E004 body references \"the detail-polling data layer introduced for the Evaluations landing page\" — plain terms, no epic ID. OK.\n\n**E005** (Implementation, P2, gated):\n\n# Pre-start versus runtime failure distinction in the evaluations job list\n\n## Description\nExtends the failed-state rendering on the EvalHub Evaluations landing page to distinguish jobs that failed before execution started (admission errors, scheduling failures, resource constraints) from jobs that ran and then failed, using status.message.message_code where available. Builds on the state differentiation (labels, icons, colors) established for the job list.\n\n## Scope\n- Mapping from status.message.message_code values to a \"Failed (not started)\" treatment vs runtime \"Failed\", using the validated enumeration of message_code values for pre-start failures (admission error, scheduling failure, resource constraint).\n- Distinct label, icon, and color for \"Failed (not started)\" vs \"Failed\", consistent with the PatternFly status pattern and the UXD-approved state specifications.\n- Fallback where message_code is ambiguous or absent: a single \"Failed\" state with status.message text displayed as supplementary detail.\n\n## Acceptance Criteria\n- Given an evaluation job failed before execution started (e.g., admission error indicated by status.message.message_code), when a user views the Evaluations landing page, then the job row displays a distinct treatment (e.g., \"Failed (not started)\") with label, icon, and color distinct from the runtime \"Failed\" state.\n- Given a job failed during execution, then the row renders the runtime \"Failed\" treatment.\n- Given message_code is ambiguous or absent for a failed job, then the row falls back to a single \"Failed\" state with status.message text as supplementary detail.\n- The new visual treatments are reviewed against the UXD state icon/color specifications.\n\n## HLR Traceability\n- [P2] Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction using status.message.message_code where available\n\n---\n\nSignal files: for each epic, a markdown table Signal/Value/Rationale.\n\nNow frontmatter sets:\n\nE001:\n```\npython3 scripts/frontmatter.py set artifacts/epic-tasks/RHAISTRAT-8005-E001.md \\\n  epic_id=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001\" title=\"Investigate mid-run availability of benchmark status in the EvalHub job detail API\" parent_strat=\"RHAISTRAT-8005\" \\\n  component=\"AI Evaluations\" team=\"TrustyAI / EvalHub\" type=Investigation priority=P1 \\\n  investigation_signals.question_specificity=1 investigation_signals.source_accessibility=1 \\\n  investigation_signals.local_runnability=0 investigation_signals.cluster_hardware_dependence=-1 \\\n  investigation_signals.human_judgment_required=0\n```\n\nE002: same, priority=P2, title=...\n\nE003:\n```\ncomponent=\"AI Core Dashboard\" team=\"ODH Dashboard\" type=Implementation priority=P0 dependencies=\"\" ...\n```\ndependencies default [] — set nothing? `dependencies` default empty list. If E003 has no deps, can omit. I'll omit.\n\nai_signals for E003: change_specificity=1 pattern_precedent=1 adapter_pattern=1 existing_foundation=1 open_questions=1 external_dependency=1 human_process_gates=-1 repo_access=1 architecture_claims=1.\n\nE004: dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001,RHAISTRAT-8005-E003\" gated_by=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E001\" gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"...\" priority=P1 ai_signals...: change_specificity=1 pattern_precedent=1 adapter_pattern=1 existing_foundation=1 open_questions=-1 external_dependency=1 human_process_gates=-1 repo_access=1 architecture_claims=1.\n\nE005: dependencies=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E002,RHAISTRAT-8005-E003\" gated_by=\"RHAISTRAT-8005-E002\" gate_failure_impact.action=remove gate_failure_impact.fallback_approach=\"...\" priority=P2, ai_signals: change_specificity=0 pattern_precedent=1 adapter_pattern=0 existing_foundation=1 open_questions=-1 external_dependency=1 human_process_gates=0 repo_access=1 architecture_claims=1.\n\nDecomposition summary body. Let me draft it fully.\n\nAlso note: \"Do not return a summary\" — my final message should be brief per the harness rules (result: line). The background-job conventions require a result: line at end.\n\nLet me now write the decomposition summary body.\n\nSections:\n- Epic List table\n- Dependency DAG mermaid\n- DAG Justification\n- HLR Traceability Matrix\n- Health Warnings\n- Tiered Delivery (state not applicable)\n- plus: Component & team mapping notes, Staff Engineer Input overrides, Investigation outcome handling (conditional branches decision), passive component handling.\n\nThe required sections are the six listed; I can add others. I'll add \"Staff Engineer Input Overrides\", \"Component Mapping\", \"Investigation Outcomes and Conditional Handling\" after DAG justification or before health warnings.\n\nLet me write everything now. First the summary body, then run frontmatter set. Then epic bodies + frontmatter + signal files. Then batch-read, validate, decompose_complete.\n\nFor the Write tool then `frontmatter.py set` — check that set prepends frontmatter to existing body. Presumably yes (used in triage path on files written by hand).\n\nOne more check: validator checks \"an epic that directly or transitively enables a P0 epic must itself be P0\". E001→E004(P1): fine. Nothing enables E003. OK.\n\nAlso Rule 11 docs — no