{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b681d4d6876d46d1","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t# AI Signals Rationale: RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 — Investigate mid-run availability of benchmark status in the EvalHub job detail API\n2\t\n3\tInvestigation epic — scored with the 5 investigation signals.\n4\t\n5\t| Signal | Value | Rationale |\n6\t|--------|-------|-----------|\n7\t| question_specificity | +1 | Concrete, enumerable question with a defined pass/fail criterion: is `status.benchmarks[]` populated with per-benchmark status entries while a job is running, or only after completion? The strategy's Open Questions table states it verbatim and the impact of each answer is defined. |\n8\t| source_accessibility | +1 | The answer is determined by readable artifacts: the EvalHub Server is an open-source Go repository (opendatahub-io/eval-hub, covered by the eval-hub.md architecture context) whose source shows when benchmark status entries are written, plus the published API reference for GET /api/v1/evaluations/jobs/{id} cited in the strategy. |\n9\t| local_runnability | 0 | The eval-hub server can start in local/development mode with SQLite, but executing a real evaluation job requires dispatching Kubernetes batch Jobs and dependent infrastructure, so the full flow cannot be exercised as a plain local binary/library. |\n10\t| cluster_hardware_dependence | -1 | The gating question is answerable from server source analysis; however, empirically corroborating a mid-run detail response requires a live evaluation job running on a cluster — a peripheral confirmation need, not a gating one. |\n11\t| human_judgment_required | 0 | Purely factual/technical question; no stakeholder or product decision and no external-party coordination is needed to resolve it. |\n12\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-b697caef81f58110","agent_id":"a5deb79688eac204b","session_id":"6801b43c-a805-4fcb-88e0-698d74311470","uuid":"da123c66-7531-4cb8-a62e-7844410