9\tedges above.\n70\t\n71\tOne minor: the summary's \"Investigation Outcomes and Conditional Handling\" section states\n72\t\"Neither outcome changes which epics exist or the DAG shape — only the internal scope of\n73\tthe gated epic changes,\" but E002's failure outcome removes E005 entirely\n74\t(`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`). The frontmatter gating metadata is correct and\n75\tconsistent; only the summary prose is self-contradictory. Not using `-BRANCH-` files is\n76\tnevertheless the right call — outcome (b) involves no distinct branch work (the fallback\n77\tis already delivered by E003's failed-state rendering), so `gated_by` + `action=remove` is\n78\tthe correct mechanism.\n79\t\n80\t### 3. Epic Boundaries (2/2)\n81\t\n82\tComponent/team tuples are cleanly separated: E001/E002 (AI Evaluations, TrustyAI / EvalHub)\n83\tinvestigate EvalHub Server behavior; E003/E004/E005 (AI Core Dashboard, ODH Dashboard)\n84\timplement the plugin UI. The passive odh-dashboard BFF verification is correctly an\n85\tacceptance criterion on E003 rather than a separate epic, per the strategy's \"no code\n86\tchange expected.\"\n87\t\n88\tNo scope duplication. The E003/E005 relationship was examined against the\n89\tsame-surface/thin-increment rule: E005 (\"Failed (not started)\" variant) is a thin increment\n90\ton E003's failed-state rendering for the same team, but it is not a priority-label-only\n91\tsplit — it is gated on Investigation E002 and removable in full\n92\t(`gate_failure_impact.action=remove`), and HLR-7 is a distinct P2 requirement, not\n93\tredundant wording of HLR-2. Merging it into P0 E003 would constitute priority collapse\n94\t(deferrable P2 work trapped in a P0 epic), which the planning requirement prohibits. The\n95\tE003/E004 split follows the data-layer/display boundary with HLR-4 explicitly apportioned\n96\talong it. No epic bundles code implementation with content authoring (UXD specification\n97\treview is captured as ACs, and the \"to be created\" UX spec is flagged in Health Warnings).\n98\tAll epics are well within ~2 weeks for an S-sized, single-package frontend change.\n99\t\n100\t### 4. Type Correctness (2/2)\n101\t\n102\tBoth Investigation epics genuinely resolve uncertainty that changes downstream structure:\n103\tE001's outcome rewrites E004's scope (benchmark progress vs elapsed-time-primary); E002's\n104\toutcome determines whether E005 exists at all. Both have downstream dependents. All three\n105\tImplementation epics produce concrete artifacts (polling hook + state UI; progress/elapsed\n106\tdisplay; failure-state distinction UI).\n107\t\n108\tGating metadata is complete and correct: E004 (`gated_by: E001`,\n109\t`gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite` with a concrete fallback approach) and E005\n110\t(`gated_by: E002`, `action=remove` with fallback). Both `gated_by` targets appear in the\n111\trespective epics' direct `dependencies` lists. No epic lists an Investigation in\n112\t`dependencies` without `gated_by`; no `gated_by` exists without `gate_failure_impact`.\n113\t\n114\t### 5. AI Implementability Scoring (2/2)\n115\t\n116\tType-appropriate signal sets are used: E001/E002 carry `investigation_signals` (5-signal\n117\trubric); E003/E004/E005 carry `ai_signals` (9-signal rubric). No epic mixes sets.\n118\t\n119\tInvestigation signals verified against the rubric: `question_specificity: 1` (both\n120\tquestions are stated verbatim in the strategy's Open Questions table with defined\n121\timpact), `source_accessibility: 1` (the answer is determined by the open-source EvalHub\n122\tServer repo and published API docs — readable artifacts that hold the answer, not a\n123\tpending human decision), `local_runnability: 0` (executing a real evaluation job requires\n124\tKubernetes batch Jobs), `cluster_hardware_dependence: -1` (rubric: \"-1 some peripheral\n125\tquestions need a live cluster\" — exactly what the rationales describe: source analysis\n126\tanswers the gating question, a live cluster only