{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"chatcmpl-tool-9c9f7327a3a8a79f","type":"tool_result","content":"1\t---\n2\tparent_strat: RHAISTRAT-8005\n3\tepic_count: 5\n4\tcritical_path_length: 2\n5\tdecompose_complete: true\n6\ttriage: null\n7\ttriage_rationale: null\n8\t---\n9\t# Decomposition Summary: RHAISTRAT-8005 — EvalHub UI: Real-Time Evaluation Job Progress and State Visibility\n10\t\n11\t## Triage Outcome\n12\t\n13\tThe strategy is S-sized, frontend-only, and owned by a single implementation team — but it carries two\n14\topen questions explicitly marked \"needs validation\" whose answers change what downstream epics do\n15\t(whether benchmark-level progress can be displayed mid-run, and whether pre-start failures can be\n16\tdistinguished from runtime failures). The genuine-unknowns escape condition therefore applies and the\n17\tstrategy is fully decomposed rather than collapsed to a single below-threshold epic.\n18\t\n19\t## Epic List\n20\t\n21\t| ID | Title | Type | Team | Priority |\n22\t|----|-------|------|------|----------|\n23\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E001 | Investigate mid-run availability of benchmark status in the EvalHub job detail API | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P1 |\n24\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E002 | Investigate message_code values distinguishing pre-start from runtime evaluation failures | Investigation | TrustyAI / EvalHub | P2 |\n25\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E003 | EvalHub Evaluations page auto-refresh polling layer and state differentiation | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P0 |\n26\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E004 | Benchmark progress and elapsed time display for running evaluation jobs | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P1 |\n27\t| RHAISTRAT-8005-E005 | Pre-start versus runtime failure distinction in the evaluations job list | Implementation | ODH Dashboard | P2 |\n28\t\n29\t## Dependency DAG\n30\t\n31\t```mermaid\n32\tgraph TD\n33\t    E001[\"E001 Inv: benchmarks[] mid-run availability\"] --> E004[\"E004 Impl: progress + elapsed display\"]\n34\t    E002[\"E002 Inv: message_code failure classes\"] --> E005[\"E005 Impl: pre-start vs runtime failure\"]\n35\t    E003[\"E003 Impl: polling layer + state differentiation\"] --> E004\n36\t    E003 --> E005\n37\t```\n38\t\n39\tRoots: E001, E002, E003 (all three can start immediately). Critical path length: 2 epics.\n40\t\n41\t## DAG Justification\n42\t\n43\t| Edge | Rule | Rationale |\n44\t|------|------|-----------|\n45\t| E001 → E004 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementation) | Whether `status.benchmarks[]` is populated mid-run determines the scope of the progress display: if only populated on completion, the epic is rewritten to elapsed-time-primary. True gate, `gate_failure_impact.action=rewrite`. |\n46\t| E002 → E005 | Rule 3 (Investigation gates affected Implementation) | Whether `message_code` values reliably distinguish pre-start from runtime failures determines whether the P2 distinction feature exists at all. True gate, `gate_failure_impact.action=remove` (fallback: single Failed state with `status.message` text, already provided by the core state differentiation). |\n47\t| E003 → E004 | Rule 13 (producer artifact → consumer serial) | The progress/elapsed display builds directly against the detail-polling hook and its fetched data (`status.benchmarks[]`, `started_at`), produced by the polling-layer epic. |\n48\t| E003 → E005 | Rule 12/13 (framework → consumer) | The failure distinction refines the failed-state rendering (label/icon/color per state) established by the state differentiation work. |\n49\t| No edge E001/E002 → E003 | — | Neither unknown affects the P0 core: list-endpoint state display, the polling mechanics, concurrency cap, retry, and page-visibility behavior are identical under every outcome. P0 work is not blocked by either investigation. |\n50\t| No edge E004 ↔ E005 | Rule 14/15 (independent surfaces) | Progress display and failure-state distinction touch different aspects of the job row and share no artifacts beyond the shared P0 foundation. |\n51\t\n52\t## HLR Traceability Matrix\n53\t\n54\t| HLR | Priority | Epic(s) |\n55\t|-----|----------|---------|\n56\t| HLR-1: Auto-refresh mechanism for the Evaluations landing page (list polling for top-level state + detail polling for in-progress job progress) | P0 | E003 |\n57\t| HLR-2: Visual distinction between queued/pending, running, failed, and completed states (labels, icons, colors from `status.state`) | P0 | E003, E005 |\n58\t| HLR-3: Non-blocking error handling during detail polling (last state preserved, inline warning, exponential backoff retry) | P0 | E003 |\n59\t| HLR-4: Benchmark-level progress display (\"Benchmarks: X/N complete\") with max-5 concurrent round-robin fetch for in-progress jobs on the current page | P1 | E003 (round-robin fetch layer), E004 (progress display) |\n60\t| HLR-5: Elapsed time display for running jobs, computed client-side from per-benchmark `started_at` | P1 | E004 |\n61\t| HLR-6: Polling pauses when the browser tab is backgrounded (Page Visibility API), resumes immediately on focus | P1 | E003 |\n62\t| HLR-7: Pre-start vs. runtime failure distinction using `status.message.message_code` | P2 | E005 |\n63\t\n64\tAll seven HLRs are covered; no orphaned requirements. HLR-4 is split across E003/E004 along the\n65\tdata-layer/display boundary: the P0 auto-refresh HLR already requires the detail-polling mechanism\n66\t(including the NFR-mandated 5-concurrent cap), so the polling layer lives in the P0 epic and the\n67\tdisplay consumes it.\n68\t\n69\t## Staff Engineer Input Overrides\n70\t\n71\tThe Staff Engineer Input section is non-empty and takes precedence. Logged divergences/adoptions:\n72\t\n73\t1. **Data availability constraint** — Staff direction: assume no data beyond what the two existing\n74\t   API endpoints provide. Adopted throughout; no Server-side extension epics are generated\n75\t   (consistent with the strategy's out-of-scope list).\n76\t2. **Polling design specifics** — 10-second